<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aristagoras of Miletus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 497 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aristagoras was tyrant of Miletus, ruling in place of his father-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/histiaeus">Histiaeus</a> under Persian authority. After a failed expedition against Naxos he led the Ionian cities into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-revolt">Ionian Revolt</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> in 499 BCE and sought help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. He left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, where he was killed, and Persia sacked Miletus in 494 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aristagoras-of-miletus.jpg" alt="Aristagoras of Miletus" width="320" height="141" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IONIA,_Miletos._5th_century_BC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 499 BCE <b>Aristagoras</b>, who governed the city for the tyrant Histiaeus, failed in a Persian-backed attack on Naxos and then led the Ionians in rebellion; with Athenian and Eretrian help the rebels burned Sardis in 498 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/miletus">Miletus · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 499 BCE <b>Aristagoras of Miletus</b> and the Persians failed to capture Naxos, a fiasco that triggered the Ionian Revolt.</blockquote><a href="/state/naxos">Naxos</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Miletus, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.53,27.28&amp;year=-507" aria-label="Miletus on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5232 813 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Aristagoras of Miletus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5682 1083h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5682 1083h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.53,27.28&amp;year=-507">What was here in 507 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
